\section{A Form Can Be Read, Expanded, and Evaluated}

The Lisp reader converts characters into objects. The evaluator or
compiler then treats those objects according to the language's form
syntax. A macro transformer consumes a form and returns another
form; it need not inspect values that will exist only when the new
form runs. Kent M. Pitman's \textit{Special Forms in Lisp} draws an
important historical contrast with FEXPRs, which can receive
unevaluated operands at execution time. When a compiler sees an
ordinary macro's expansion before generating code, jumps and
bindings that were implicit in the surface form become explicit.
Run-time access to operands and their environment imposes a
different implementation burden.

Christian Queinnec's \textit{Lisp in Small Pieces} works through
interpreters that make binding environments and control explicit.
Nils M. Holm's \textit{Lisp from Nothing} uses a sequence of small
implementations to get from an evaluator to a system with a macro
expander. This separation gives a practical build rule: provide the
expander with the definitions it needs at expansion time. For
example, a Common Lisp \texttt{defmacro} that calls a helper defined
only for load time may fail in a fresh compile even if it works in
a long-running session. Make the helper available in the compilation
environment, for example by an earlier-loaded dependency or an
appropriate top-level \texttt{eval-when}. See Chapter~43 of
\textit{The Modern Compiler Dossier} for a related proposal about
tracking the stage at which a dependency is available.

The reader's output need not represent a Lisp program. In
\textit{Implementing S-Expression Based Extended Languages in
Lisp}, Tasuku Hiraishi, Masahiro Yasugi, and Taiichi Yuasa
use Lisp transformations over an S-expression representation
of extended C-like languages. The host reader and tree
machinery can therefore be reused without importing Lisp
evaluation into the target. A transformer for such a language
still has to preserve target scoping and evaluation order;
sharing a tree representation does not establish semantic
equivalence.

\section{Worked Example: Evaluate Once Without Capturing a Name}

Paul Graham's \textit{On Lisp} treats unintended capture and
repeated evaluation as different faults. In the following macro,
\texttt{gensym} produces an uninterned symbol for a binding that
cannot conflict with a caller's variable just because of its
spelling. The expression is inserted once, in the initializer.

\begin{verbatim}
(defmacro unless-present (form alternative)
  (let ((value (gensym "VALUE-")))
    `(let ((,value ,form))
       (if ,value ,value ,alternative))))

(let ((value 41) (calls 0))
  (list (unless-present (progn (incf calls) nil)
                        (1+ value))
        calls)) ; => (42 1)
\end{verbatim}

The alternative refers to the caller's \texttt{value}, not to an
introduced binding. A complementary test changes the first operand
to return a nonfalse value and puts an error in the alternative:
the error must not occur and the operand must still run exactly
once. The intended contract here is for the primary value, not
preservation of all multiple values. Looking at the result of
\texttt{macroexpand-1} tests the binding shape, while running the
two inputs checks the conditional evaluation policy. Neither test
should rely on an exact printed name for the generated symbol.

\section{Reflection Is More Than Calling Eval}

In Common Lisp, \texttt{eval} operates in the current dynamic
environment and the null lexical environment; it does not inherit
arbitrary lexical bindings of its caller. A quoted symbol named
\texttt{x} passed from a lexical \texttt{let} does not carry the
binding of \texttt{x}. A closure can preserve such a binding because
it was created in the appropriate environment, as Holm explains
with Scheme examples in \textit{Sketchy LISP}. This distinction
extends the evaluator discussion of Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-151}.

Peter Lee and Uwe Pleban explain another boundary in \textit{On
the Use of Lisp in Implementing Denotational Semantics}.
Ordinary evaluation is not a partial evaluator. If an input is
unavailable, calling \texttt{eval} on an expression that needs it
does not generally simplify the portions independent of that
input and return residual code. Their Scheme-based MESS compiler
generator exploits the structure of the semantic descriptions
to avoid relying on such simplification by the evaluator.

Brian Cantwell Smith's \textit{Reflection and Semantics in Lisp}
goes further in 3-Lisp. Its tower of connected interpreters lets
programs make currently implicit evaluation state explicit. A
reflective procedure can work with representations of an environment
and a continuation, making it possible to define control operations
in terms resembling their semantic equations. The tower is a
semantic description, not a requirement to run infinitely many
interpreters in lockstep. In contrast, a normal source transformer
works with program forms and transforms future execution; it does
not have access to the continuation of the eventual call site.
Keeping those powers separate gives both the compiler and the
programmer a boundary they can reason about.
